\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A data manifold is a proposed lower-dimensional geometric support for observations in a larger ambient space. Local entropy and correlations measure chosen features of nearby distributions. Nonlinear principal components seek a reduced representation beyond a linear subspace.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The manifold regularity, sampling law, identifiability conditions, and performance criterion are not fixed.

Source-record limitation: The data model, manifold assumptions, and learning or augmentation guarantee are unspecified.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can the underlying data manifold be learned using local entropy and correlation, can this improve data augmentation, and can nonlinear principal components exploit the manifold hypothesis? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2024/21}{[S2]}
